Yes—though I’d frame it as expanding your body’s available states, not forcing yourself into one “correct” posture.

Anterior pelvic tilt can contribute to the visual effect you’re describing, especially when it comes with rib flare + lumbar extension + weak control of the pelvis relative to the rib cage. But a lower-abdominal bulge is not proof of anterior pelvic tilt by itself. Fat distribution, abdominal wall shape, bowel fullness/bloating, rib position, breathing mechanics, genetics, and how you habitually stand all affect it.

For you, I’d train stacking and control, not just “more abs.”

The position you’re after

Think:

ribs over pelvis → pelvis neutral enough to move both directions → abdominal wall engaged without sucking in → glutes available → relaxed breathing.

A lot of strong people get very good at living in extension:

chest up → ribs flare → lower back arches → pelvis tips forward → abdomen gets pushed forward.

That can look like a belly even when you’re lean.

The correction is not walking around aggressively tucking your pelvis under. You want to regain the middle.

Your highest-return exercises

1. 90/90 wall breathing — probably #1 for you

Lie on your back with hips and knees at roughly 90°, feet against a wall.

Gently tilt your pelvis so your low back becomes heavier against the floor. Don’t mash it flat.

Then take a slow breath through your nose and make the exhale long.

During the exhale, feel your ribs come down and inward, while the lower abdomen firms gently.

Do 4–5 breaths × 2–3 sets.

This teaches:

rib cage + diaphragm + abdominal wall + pelvis = one coordinated cylinder.

And importantly, it doubles as down-regulation work.

⸻

2. Dead bug with full exhale

Don’t treat it as an ab contest.

Get the rib/pelvis position first.

Exhale → ribs down → gently brace → slowly extend opposite arm/leg without letting your lower back arch.

3 × 5–8 controlled reps per side.

If your back arches, you’ve reached the edge of your usable range.

⸻

3. Reverse crunch / pelvic curl

This is different from throwing your legs around.

Bring your knees toward your ribs by curling the pelvis slightly off the ground.

Think:

pubic bone toward sternum, not “knees toward face.”

2–3 × 8–15.

This teaches your abdominal wall to actually control pelvic position.

⸻

4. Glute bridge with pelvic control

Before lifting:

slight posterior pelvic tilt → ribs down → squeeze glutes → lift.

If you feel mostly low back or hamstrings, reset.

3 × 8–12 with a slow tempo.

Eventually progress to single-leg versions.

⸻

5. Side plank

Your core isn’t merely six-pack musculature.

The obliques are extremely important for controlling the relationship between the ribs and pelvis.

2–3 × 20–45 sec each side.

Eventually add carries, Pallof presses, etc.

⸻

Then strengthen the posture dynamically

Once you own the basic position:

Goblet squats
Split squats
Front-foot elevated split squats
Romanian deadlifts
Suitcase carries
Farmer carries

The instruction across all of them is:

don’t turn “chest up” into “rib cage launched toward the ceiling.”

Stay tall without hyperextending.

That one cue could make a surprisingly large difference.

Stretch what is actually pulling you toward extension

The obvious target is the hip flexor complex, but technique matters.

For a half-kneeling hip-flexor stretch:

Get into a lunge.

Before moving forward, slightly tuck the pelvis and squeeze the glute of the kneeling leg.

Now shift forward only a little.

You should feel the front of that hip/thigh.

If you’re creating a giant lumbar arch, you’ve turned a hip-flexor stretch into a low-back stretch.

Do 45–60 sec × 2 each side.

The couch stretch is also good, but use the same pelvic position.

I’d also work:

* quads
* adductors
* lats
* thoracic spine
* calves/ankles

because posture is a whole-chain problem, not merely “tight hip flexors.”

One thing I’d not do

Don’t spend fifteen minutes aggressively stretching your hamstrings just because someone says APT means your hamstrings are “tight.”

They can feel tight because they’re already lengthened and working hard to resist the forward pelvic position.

Sometimes those hamstrings need better positioning and strengthening—not endless stretching.

And yes, train your abs—but differently

You could have extremely strong abs and still stand in a position where they don’t control your rib cage and pelvis very well.

For your particular goal, I’d prioritize:

obliques + transverse abdominal control + rectus abdominal control + breathing coordination

over doing hundreds of traditional crunches.

And I’d especially train anti-extension:

dead bug
body saw
ab wheel eventually
planks
hollow-position variations

because the movement you are trying to control is excessive lumbar extension.

Your 10-minute daily reset

Do this after your dog walk or before bed:

2 min — 90/90 breathing
2 min — hip-flexor/couch stretch
2 min — dead bugs
2 min — glute bridges
1 min — side plank left
1 min — side plank right

Do that most days for a month while continuing your normal lifting.

And while walking, experiment—not rigidly—with:

head floating upward → ribs relaxed down → belt buckle roughly level → glutes relaxed → breathe into the whole torso.

Not military attention.

In fact, that’s probably an important distinction for you:

good posture isn’t standing harder.

It is having enough strength, mobility and nervous-system control that you don’t have to hold yourself rigidly.

And yes, I think your abundance analogy actually works here. A body stuck in fight-or-flight has relatively few available responses: brace, clench, accelerate, protect.

A more adaptable body has more movement options:

brace or soften
extend or flex
produce force or absorb it
move or remain still
inhale fully and exhale fully

So in that sense, physical abundance is degrees of freedom.

You’re not trying to exchange a “bad posture” for another posture prison. You’re increasing the number of comfortable, controllable states your body can occupy.

